{-
   A function as a **value** - Analysis-IR.md §11.4's G7, and the row `GenCallable` said this stage
   did not need.

   A callable *requirement* - `Callable.yana` - is a shape to select a function by, and it is
   discharged where the call is written. This is the other thing: a value, held in a local, stored
   in a field, handed to a callee that has never heard of `twice`, and called through whatever it
   turned out to hold. What decides which body runs is the pair itself, and the pair is what a
   position of type `(I32) -> I32` accepts.

   The representation is **two words** - a code address and the environment its captures live in -
   and `code` always takes the environment as its first argument. That is what makes a function type
   a type rather than a family of them: a call site holding one never has to know whether it came
   from a name or from a lambda. `lower_fun.cpp` is where both ends of that are written, and the one
   cost it has is visible in the golden: a plain function goes through a `funvalue(...)` thunk that
   absorbs the environment argument, because a declaration has its own convention.

   Four things this asserts that `Callable.yana` cannot:

    - the type is **structural**. `apply` and `Handler` write `(I32) -> I32` independently and mean
      one type, which is what lets a value cross between them;
    - a value survives a **record**. `Handler {run: f, times: 2}` puts the pair in a field and
      `dispatch` reads it back out and calls it, so the two words travel as data rather than as a
      call the compiler can see through;
    - a **generic** body can hold and call one. `twiceOver` is written over `a` and calls a value
      whose type mentions no variable at all;
    - and so can a body **compiled once**. `@generic` on `twiceOver` is what asserts that: a
      function value is 16 bytes whatever its signature, so an erased body needs no descriptor for
      it, and the call is two loads and an indirect call that depend on no type parameter.

   A fifth thing is one line rather than a section: the arity comes from the *position*, so
   `apply(twice, v)` needs no annotation and `let f = twice` needs one. `Reject.FunValue.yana` is
   where the second half of that is asserted.

   The answer is arithmetic rather than a trace, because nothing here owns anything: what a wrong
   answer would mean is the wrong body reached, and every one of the three functions below returns a
   different multiple of what it is given.
-}

fn twice(x: I32) -> I32 = x + x

fn thrice(x: I32) -> I32 = x + x + x

-- The value crossing a boundary that names no declaration: `apply` knows a shape and nothing else.
fn apply(f: (I32) -> I32, v: I32) -> I32 = f(v)

-- And crossing one as *data*. The pair is a field, read back out and called - which is the case a
-- caller cannot see through even in principle.
data Handler {run: (I32) -> I32, times: I32}

fn dispatch(h: Handler, v: I32) -> I32 = h.run(v) + h.times

{-
   A body compiled **once** that calls a function value.

   `@generic` rather than measured, because what is being asserted is that the erased form can do
   this at all: the value's own type mentions no type parameter, so the pair is the same 16 bytes in
   every instantiation and the call reads it without touching the environment the body was handed.
-}
@generic
fn (a.tag: I32) twiceOver(x: a, f: (I32) -> I32) -> I32 = f(x.tag)

data Label {tag: I32}

-- The hint reaching an argument, which is what makes `apply(twice, ...)` writable at all: a value
-- position picks the arity, and an argument's position is its parameter's declared type.
fn twiceApplied(v: I32) -> I32 = apply(twice, v)

fn main() -> I32:
    let f = twice :: (I32) -> I32
    let g = thrice :: (I32) -> I32
    let h = Handler {run: g, times: 5 :: I32}
    let l = Label {tag: 7 :: I32}
    return apply(f, 10 :: I32) + dispatch(h, 100 :: I32) + twiceOver(l, f) + twiceApplied(1 :: I32)
